.TH NOVA 1 "June 2026" "nova 0.1.0" "Nova Manual"
.SH NAME
nova \- self-hosting Nova programming language compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nova
.RI [ options ]
.IR source.nova \ \-o\  out.s
.PP
.B nova \-\-version
.PP
.B nova \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nova
compiles Nova source files to native machine code via GAS assembly.
The compiler emits architecture-specific assembly that is then handed
off to
.B as
and
.B ld
(or the cross-toolchain equivalent for the selected target).
.PP
Nova is self-hosting: the compiler is written in Nova and bootstraps
itself in two stages through a minimal assembly trampoline at
.IR boot/nova_boot.s .
There is no libc dependency at runtime; the compiler issues Linux
syscalls directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the compiler version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the emitted assembly to
.IR file .
The default target is the native Linux x86-64 ABI.
.TP
.BI \-\-target= name
Cross-compile for an alternate target. Recognised names include
.IR linux ,
.IR windows ,
.IR windows\-arm64 ,
.IR macos ,
.IR arm64 ,
.IR wasm .
.TP
.B \-\-link\-libc
Emit a startup stub that links against libc / libdl for FFI support.
The default is a libc-free direct-syscall runtime.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/nova
Compiler binary.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/nova/examples/
Example Nova programs shipped with the package.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/nova.1
This manual page.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile and run a hello-world program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nova hello.nova \-o /tmp/hello.s
as \-o /tmp/hello.o /tmp/hello.s
ld \-o /tmp/hello /tmp/hello.o
/tmp/hello
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cross-compile to Windows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nova hello.nova \-\-target=windows \-o /tmp/hello.s
x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-as \-o /tmp/hello.o /tmp/hello.s
x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-ld \-o hello.exe /tmp/hello.o \-lkernel32 \-lmsvcrt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR as (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR gcc (1).
.PP
